### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

router

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

When a navigation is cancelled or fails after its URL has already been committed, `NavigationStateManager.cancel()` puts the previous URL back with `navigation.navigate(previousUrl, {history: 'replace', info: {ɵrouterInfo: {intercept: false}}})`. `handleNavigate` then returns early for that event without intercepting it. A navigation that nobody intercepts is a regular one, so the browser loads the previous URL as a new document and the whole app restarts.

I could reproduce it in these cases, all with the default `canceledNavigationResolution: 'replace'`:

- Chrome, default config: a routed component throws in its constructor (an `inject()` without a provider, for example). In `'deferred'` mode the URL is committed right before activation, so the error goes through the rollback.
- Chrome with `urlUpdateStrategy: 'eager'`: any guard that rejects.
- Safari 26.6, which doesn't support `precommitHandler`, so the URL is committed as soon as the event is intercepted: a guard that rejects asynchronously after a plain link click, the back button on a page whose `canDeactivate` asks for confirmation and gets "no", and stopping a navigation in progress (`window.stop()`).

The `canDeactivate` case is the worst one: the guard is there to keep unsaved changes, and the reload throws them away.

With the History API integration, the same rollback is a `replaceState()` and the page stays. The router's tests expect that for both integrations ("should dispatch NavigationError after the url has been reset back" and "can renavigate to throwing component" run with the Navigation API too). They pass there only because `FakeNavigation` never loads a new document.

With `canceledNavigationResolution: 'computed'`, rollbacks after a push navigation use `traverseTo()` instead, which stays in the same document, so most of these cases don't reload there.

#71597 intercepts the rollback navigation, without a handler, so only the URL changes.

### Please provide a link to a minimal reproduction of the bug

No hosted reproduction, but this is self-contained:

```ts
import {Component} from '@angular/core';
import {bootstrapApplication} from '@angular/platform-browser';
import {
  RouterLink,
  RouterOutlet,
  provideRouter,
  withExperimentalPlatformNavigation,
} from '@angular/router';

@Component({template: ''})
class Home {}

@Component({template: ''})
class Broken {
  constructor() {
    throw new Error('Broken');
  }
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [RouterLink, RouterOutlet],
  template: `
    <p>Loaded at {{ loadedAt }}</p>
    <a routerLink="/broken">Go to a broken page</a>
    <router-outlet />
  `,
})
class App {
  loadedAt = new Date().toLocaleTimeString();
}

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    provideRouter(
      [
        {path: '', component: Home},
        {path: 'broken', component: Broken},
      ],
      withExperimentalPlatformNavigation(),
    ),
  ],
});
```

In Chrome, click the link. The error is logged, then the page reloads: "Loaded at" changes and the server gets a new request for `/`. Without `withExperimentalPlatformNavigation()`, the URL goes back to `/` and the page stays as it is.

### Please provide the exception or error you saw

```true
No error from the rollback itself, the page just reloads.
```

### Please provide the environment you discovered this bug in (run `ng version`)

```true
Angular 22.2.1, Chrome 155 and Safari 26.6.2. Current main has the same code.
```

### Anything else?

One related thing in Safari that the fix doesn't cover: when a guard rejects synchronously after a plain link click or the back button, `cancel()` runs while the `navigate` event is still being dispatched. At that point the URL hasn't changed yet, so there's nothing to roll back, but the browser commits the intercepted navigation right after. The URL ends up on the rejected route while the router stays on the previous page. That's a different code path, so I left it out.

### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

core

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

When a resource's `params` function throws (for example a thrown validation error, or mirroring an
upstream resource's error), the resource correctly reports `status() === 'error'`. Calling `reload()`
in that state breaks the resource:

- `reload()` returns `true`, but the loader never runs,
- `status()` switches from `'error'` to `'idle'`,
- `error()` still returns the old error while the status claims `'idle'`,
- every subsequent `reload()` returns `false` (the `'idle'` guard), so a "Retry" button is
  permanently dead.

The cause is in `ResourceImpl.reload()` (`packages/core/src/resource/resource.ts:407`):

```ts
this.extRequest.update(({request, reload}) => ({request, reload: reload + 1}));
```

`extRequest` is a union: after a params error its value is `{error, reload}`, with no `request`
field. Destructuring `request` yields `undefined` and the rebuilt object drops `error`, so the state
computation maps `request === undefined` to `'idle'` (resource.ts:320) while reusing the previous —
error — stream (resource.ts:321), which is why `error()` keeps reporting the stale error.

Expected behavior: `reload()` should return `false` and leave the error state untouched when the
error came from `params` (only a params change can recover it), or alternatively preserve the error
state instead of fabricating `'idle'`. Returning `true` without loading, and reporting `'idle'`
while `error()` is set, are both incoherent.

## Minimal reproduction (TestBed spec)

```ts
import {ApplicationRef, Injector, resource, signal} from '@angular/core';
import {TestBed} from '@angular/core/testing';

it('reload() while params is in error', async () => {
  const s = signal<string | Error>(new Error('params failed'));
  let loads = 0;
  const res = resource({
    params: () => {
      const v = s();
      if (v instanceof Error) throw v;
      return v;
    },
    loader: async ({params}) => {
      loads++;
      return params;
    },
    injector: TestBed.inject(Injector),
  });
  await TestBed.inject(ApplicationRef).whenStable();

  expect(res.status()).toBe('error'); // ✓

  const result = res.reload();
  await TestBed.inject(ApplicationRef).whenStable();

  console.log(result);        // true  — claims a reload happened
  console.log(res.status());  // 'idle' — was 'error'
  console.log(res.error());   // Error: params failed — still set while 'idle'
  console.log(loads);         // 0     — loader never ran
  console.log(res.reload());  // false — retry permanently dead
});
```

Observed on `main` (`c27695f278`, 22.3.0-next.2); the relevant code is unchanged in 22.x stable.


